# ADDENDUM — §3 REPLACED, §9 REVISED

## ⚠ CORRECTION to what I sent earlier

In §3 I wrote *"Schechter CHI 2009: 89 participants → 4 succeeded, 52 failed, 33 never got a response from trustees."* **That is wrong and must be struck.** The 4/52/33 split is **Experiment 2 — the behaviour of 89 *trustees* under a forged-email attack**, not participants attempting recovery. I conflated two experiments. The corrected figures are below. My "4.5% success rate" claim was a fabrication of that conflation; the real completion rate is **40%**. Everything else in my §3 stands.

---

## 3 (REPLACEMENT) — SOCIAL RECOVERY AS PRACTICED

### 3a. Schechter, Egelman & Reeder, CHI 2009 — still the only end-to-end measurement, 17 years on
Deployed as a real-looking feature at recover.live.com; 4 trustees, 3-of-4; **trustees were not told they were in a study.** [PDF](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/paper1459-schechter.pdf)

**Experiment 1 — reliability. The funnel, from 43 invitees:**

| Attrition step | Lost | Left |
|---|---|---|
| Never loaded the instructions page | −13 | 30 |
| Never responded to any survey request (**despite a $10 gift card just for replying**) | −4 | 26 |
| **Couldn't remember who their trustees were**, and didn't look them up | −2 | 24 |
| Too busy to attempt the task | −2 | 22 |
| Worried trustees would fear viruses in the emails | −1 | 21 |
| Never spoke with any trustee | −2 | 19 |
| Configured **invalid emails for all trustees** | −1 | 18 |
| Only reached 2 of the required 3 trustees | −1 | **17** |

- The paper's headline — *"seventeen of the nineteen participants who made the effort to reach one trustee (89%) were able to authenticate"* — **is conditional.** The unconditional rate is **17/43 = 40% of enrolled users completed recovery.**
- **18 of 19 (94%)** would have succeeded at a relaxed 2-of-4.
- **72% (13 of 18) of the participants who SUCCEEDED had to be reminded who their own trustees were** — days after enrollment.
- Timing: **47% under 100 min · 66% within 1 day · 88% within 2 days.**
- Paper's own stated lesson: *"users must be reminded of who their trustees are."*

**Experiment 2 — forged-email attack on trustees (attack emails co-designed with Dan Kaminsky), 89 trustees reached:** 4 (4%) sent a recovery code to the attacker; **52 (58%) broke channel and contacted the account holder instead**; 33 (37%) never responded. Modelled attacker success against the live system: **0.46% at a 2-code threshold, 0.019% at 3.**

**Experiment 3 — a phone call from someone close to the victim, 42 usable calls:** **19 (45%) yielded a recovery code or trustee-authentication email.** Refusals: 12% unwilling, 33% *unable*, 10% too confused. **56% (14/25) when the caller personally knew the trustee vs 29% (5/17) when not.** Authors' caveat: mostly spouses and long-term friends, so this *"reflects the upper bound for attack efficacy."*

> **The single most load-bearing fact in this field: guardians resist a cold email (4%) and fold to a warm phone call (45%).** Google prices that call at *"on the order of $750 USD"* per account.

### 3b. The founding paper has no data
Brainard, Juels, Rivest, Szydlo & Yung, **"Fourth-Factor Authentication: Somebody You Know", ACM CCS 2006** — the originating paper of guardian recovery — contains **no empirical user data at all**: *"We are presently in the process of conducting a small pilot study."* Schechter, citing it three years later: *"To our knowledge, no usability results have been made available."* Twenty years on, that is still true.

### 3c. Loopring — the only guardian-recovery breach published **with denominators**
[Postmortem, 2024-10-11](https://medium.com/@byron.loopring/postmortem-incident-report-and-path-forward-aa64f7e5f7d1)
- 2024-04-24: operator keys compromised via `secretManager`; an admin key *"was not strictly managed, and several internal members had access to it."* Loopring reset keys but *"did not conduct a thorough review of the backend environment."*
- 2024-06-09: **the 2FA server itself was compromised.** The attacker *"temporarily replaced affected users' 2FA data with their own email addresses,"* initiated social recovery, received the code, and Loopring's relayer *"signed 'YES' to the social recovery request."* **The attacker did not defeat recovery — they invoked it, exactly as designed.**
- **Denominators, verbatim: *"Out of 40,000+ Loopring Smart Wallets currently deployed, 58 Loopring Smart Wallet addresses lost assets"*** — ~0.145%, ~$5M.
- **Root cause is the adoption failure, stated by the vendor:** *"Most impacted wallets relied solely on the default Loopring Official Guardian, which only uses users' 2FA information for verification."*
- **Correlated-guardian edge case:** *"Four wallets with more than one guardian were also compromised because the second guardian (another Loopring Smart Wallet) had already been compromised, also relying on the default Loopring Official Guardian only."*
- Singapore police declined the report (no local victims, US server). **No reimbursement**; the company pivoted to "Loopring DeFi." Contracts repo last pushed 2024-10-25.

### 3d. Argent — the vendor's own docs collapse the "social" quorum to 1-of-1
[Guardian FAQ, live](https://support.argent.xyz/hc/en-us/articles/360007981757-Ethereum-account-guardian-FAQ), verbatim:
> *"**You only need one guardian for a recovery.**"* · *"**One guardian is sufficient** … however with more guardian approvals at once, **the transaction might become more expensive fees-wise**."*

The default guardian **is Argent**, verified by SMS. Recovery: **48-hour delay**; 1 guardian → 1 approval; 2–3 → majority; 4+ → ⌈N/2⌉. The doc **does not address unavailable guardians** — it says email support.

**Did Argent abandon social recovery?** Not formally. But: `argentlabs/argent-contracts` (the guardian suite, 600 stars) **last pushed 2024-07-29 — ~2 years idle**; the company rebranded to **Ready** in late 2025; Ready's live blog has **no social-recovery messaging**; and **the 2020 guardian manifesto that Argent's own support FAQ still links to as the canonical explainer now 404s.** It was quietly de-emphasized, not announced dead.

### 3e. Safe — the social tier was never built
[Safe's own help doc](https://help.safe.global/articles/9622260218-account-recovery-with-saferecoveryhub): *"As of December 2023 the only implemented solution is a decentralized custom solution."* A **"Recoverer" is a single account — no n-of-m threshold at all**: *"Recoverers can bypass any threshold and propose ownership changes."* The only defence is a delay window. Safe's own admission: *"There is no centralized authority to help you in case the Recoverer's key is lost"* — **the recovery mechanism has the same loss problem as the thing it recovers.** Underlying primitive `zodiac-modifier-delay`: **31 stars.** No usage numbers ever published.

### 3f. ERC-4337 / account abstraction — the Ethereum Foundation's own SDK has 8 stars
[PSE, "Social Recovery SDK"](https://pse.dev/blog/social-recovery-sdk), verbatim: *"Social recovery has been discussed for years, but **developers still lack a practical, trustless SDK**"* … *"including Vitalik Buterin's writing on social recovery in January 2021, **yet there is still no simple, trustless, developer-friendly SDK**."* Repo created 2026-01-12, last push 2026-03-02, **8 stars**. Timelocks declared non-optional: *"without them, a colluding guardian set can potentially take over a wallet before the owner can react."* Three open problems, **all human-coordination, none cryptographic**: manual owner↔guardian salt handoff; DKIM registry rotation (*"DNSSEC coverage is still incomplete across providers"*); and cross-chain config (*"users must reconfigure and repeat recovery setup across chains … **affects real adoption**"*). Adjacent `zkemail/email-recovery`: **61 stars, idle ~14 months.**

**And the gap that explains everything:** Vitalik recommended **7+ guardians**; Argent shipped a default of **1** and told users more costs gas. Practitioners called it on day one — the [HN thread](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=25722131) (129 pts) predicted guardians converge on institutions: *"All roads lead to banks."* **HN carries essentially no other discussion of social recovery in its entire archive.**

### 3g. Facebook Trusted Contacts — the largest deployment ever built, killed
Launched 2011, relaunched 2013, ~**2 billion accounts**, 3-friend confirmation. **Removed 2022.** Facebook's own help-page notice, verbatim: *"Trusted contacts is going away soon. This means your chosen friends will no longer be able to help you get back on Facebook if you lose access to your account."* Replacement advice: keep your email and phone current. **Meta ran the largest possible experiment in social recovery for ~9 years, published no results, and shut it down.** *(This supersedes my earlier 16/20 — the verbatim notice was retrieved.)*

### 3h. Signal SVR — measured cost, unmeasured forgetting
SVR3 (Connell et al., **USENIX OSDI 2024**): **$0.0025/user/year**, **365 ms** to recover, *"millions of real users in a deployment with capacity for over 500 million users."* n=3 trust domains across AWS Nitro / Intel SGX / AMD SEV-SNP, s=2 supermajority, 7 servers.
**The design admission, verbatim:** the system *"does not support recovering the user PIN … **if a user forgets their PIN, they cannot recover their key**."* **Mitigation, verbatim:** *"We mitigate this in practice by **periodically prompting the user to re-enter their PIN**."*
**Signal has never published a PIN-forgetting rate** — not in 2019, not in 2020, not in the OSDI paper. All three were checked. **The engineered periodic re-prompt is the strongest indirect evidence the rate was high enough to build around.**
Matthew Green's [critique](https://blog.cryptographyengineering.com/2020/07/10/a-few-thoughts-about-signals-secure-value-recovery/): *"The key weakness of the SVR approach is that it depends strongly on the security and integrity of SGX execution"* — memory dump → offline dictionary attack whose difficulty rests *"entirely on the strength of a user's password"* (Signal encouraged **4-digit** PINs); retry-limit bypass; attestation-key leakage forcing replication into simulated non-SGX enclaves. On consent: *"My major issue with SVR is consent"* — he calls the rollout a *"dark pattern."*

### 3i. Apple — where the recovery path became the attack
- **Recovery Contacts:** up to **5**; the contact relays a **six-digit code**; *"Your recovery contacts won't have any access to your account."* **No published success rate, no delay figure.**
- **Recovery Key:** a 28-character code that **turns off Apple's standard recovery path entirely.**
- **Advanced Data Protection** requires at least one of the two; **no adoption number ever published**; Apple **withdrew ADP from the UK** in Feb–Mar 2025 rather than comply with a Technical Capability Notice.
- **The failure that actually happened (WSJ, Feb & Apr 2023):** thieves shoulder-surfed passcodes **in bars at night**, stole the phone, reset the Apple ID password in Settings, disabled Find My, **then set a Recovery Key** — permanently locking out the owner. *"Apple's policy gives users virtually no way back into their accounts without that recovery key."* **The thief did not defeat the Recovery Key; the thief set one.**
- Apple's fix, **iOS 17.3 (Jan 2024)**: Stolen Device Protection — biometrics with *"no passcode alternative or fallback"*, a **one-hour security delay** then a **second** biometric check before an Apple ID password change. **Crucial limit: only away from "familiar locations"** unless the user opts into always-on.

### 3j. WhatsApp and Bitcoin collaborative custody — all denominators absent
- **WhatsApp E2E backups (2021-09-10):** HSM "Backup Key Vault"; password or 64-digit key; the vault renders the key *"permanently inaccessible after a limited number of unsuccessful attempts."* **Meta does not state the attempt count anywhere.** The widely-repeated "5 attempts" figure **could not be verified and should not be cited.** **No adoption number has ever been published.**
- **Casa:** $21/mo 2-of-3, $175/mo 3-of-5; self-reported *"$10+ Billion Protected"*, *"40,000+ Clients."* **"Casa Covenant" no longer exists as a product** — it is now "Casa Inheritance" with a **six-month verification window.** The oft-cited "X% of Casa clients have lost a key" statistic **could not be located** despite checking the blog archive to 2018. Casa's guidance is a key health-check *"about once every six months"* — **advice, not a mechanism.**
- **Unchained:** *"$0 Capital losses"*, *"100,000+ BTC"* — self-reported, no methodology. **No key-loss or recovery data.**
- **Nunchuk** is the only vendor publishing an honest caveat — *"it's impossible to know exactly how many of these losses are due to failed inheritance plans"* — **and the only design found anywhere that treats vendor mortality as a requirement**: a Miniscript on-chain timelock "Failsafe" that works if Nunchuk disappears.
- **The descriptor problem is real:** Bitcoin Optech #351 (2025-04-25) covers a proposal for standardized encrypted wallet-descriptor backup. **The existence of that proposal is the evidence: holding your seed is not sufficient — losing the descriptor loses the funds.**

### 3k. Two folklore numbers to stop citing
- **"~20% of BTC is lost"** traces via Wikipedia to a **2018 WSJ article**, not Chainalysis; the Chainalysis URLs 404. **Vitalik's 2021 post repeats it with no citation.** This unsourced number has been the motivating statistic for the entire social-recovery program.
- **Krombholz FC 2016's "22% lost money"** is **secondhand** (via a 2018 citing paper); the primary PDF is unreachable. *(This lowers my earlier 15/20 to ~8/20 — do not cite it.)*

---

## 9 (REVISED) — NEGATIVE RESULTS

Now much stronger, and each was hunted for specifically:

1. **No wallet vendor has ever published what fraction of users configure guardians.** Not Argent, not Loopring, not Safe.
2. **No wallet vendor has ever published how many recoveries were performed, attempted, or abandoned.** **Zero recovery-success data exists for any production smart-contract wallet.**
3. **No guardian non-response rate has ever been published by any deployment.**
4. **No longitudinal study exists** on whether guardian-enrolled users can recover months or years later. Schechter's window was *days*. **This gap has stood open for 17 years.** Partially closed from an unexpected direction: Bonneau/Google's 40% at 11M-claim scale.
5. **Signal has never published a PIN-forgetting rate**, despite engineering a re-prompt against it.
6. **WhatsApp has never published an E2E-backup adoption number or its attempt threshold.**
7. **Apple has never published a Recovery Contact success rate or an ADP adoption number.**
8. **Casa / Unchained / Nunchuk publish no key-loss or recovery-exercise rates** — only "$0 losses" marketing.
9. **No Buterin retrospective revisiting the 2021 claims against outcomes was found** (medium confidence; search access degraded).
10. **No documented real-world incident of an attacker socially engineering a *human* guardian into approving a recovery.** Every documentable incident attacked the **vendor-operated** guardian (Loopring's 2FA server) or the **device passcode** (Apple). *(This is the sharpest negative in the set: the human-guardian threat is measured only in a 2009 lab, never in the wild — because in the wild there were almost no human guardians to attack.)*
11. **No academic study empirically measures usage or success rates of guardian-based social-recovery crypto wallets.** OpenAlex across SOUPS/CHI/USENIX/PETS/CCS/FC/NDSS surfaces only scheme proposals and surveys.
12. **Facebook published no outcome data in nine years and none when killing it.**
13. **(Unchanged) The civic-ceremonies lane did not hand back** — CT logs, Debian/Fedora/kernel.org key rollovers, Tor DirAuth quorum incidents, and the ICANN Ceremony 40 lock-failure verification remain unresearched.

---

## What this changes about the fork

**Nothing about the cryptography — and that is the point.** Not one of the eleven documented failures above turned on Shamir-versus-multisig. They turned on: nobody enrolled real guardians; the "social" guardian was the vendor; the recovery path became the attack path; users forgot who their guardians were; and the vendor left.

Two additions to the record that bear directly on your ruling:

- **The delay window plus an out-of-band owner alert is the load-bearing control, not the threshold.** It is the one thing that worked across every incident class — Argent 48h, Apple 1h+biometric, Safe's rejection window, and Schechter's **58% of attacked trustees who broke channel to warn the victim.** And Schechter's completion distribution (88% within 2 days) says **48 hours covers essentially the whole legitimate distribution.** This maps exactly onto ICN's R9.6 wall-clock veto in my §7 — two independent lines of evidence converging on the same number.
- **Guardian liveness is the unbuilt mechanism.** Three independent measurements point the same way — Schechter's 72%, Google's 40% across 11M claims, Signal's engineered re-prompt — and **no wallet ships a check.** Under Fork B this is nearly free: challenge each guardian to sign a nonce, on a schedule. Under Fork A a card cannot be proven live without reconstructing. **That asymmetry is decision-relevant and I had it at lower confidence before this handback.**

Finally, on threshold choice: Schechter measured **3-of-4 → 89% reliability at 0.019% attack success**, versus **2-of-4 → 94% at 0.46%**. Five points of reliability for a 24× increase on a still-tiny attack probability. The paper's own suggestion — **start strict, relax for dormant accounts** — is the mirror image of ICANN's rule that recovery be *stricter* than operation, and the two are reconcilable only by deciding which failure you fear more. That is a ruling, and it is yours.
