# FINAL ADDENDUM — §5 REPLACED, §7 EXTENDED

## ⚠ TWO CORRECTIONS to what I sent earlier

**1. I said "in the world's most audited key ceremony, the 'guardian is unreachable' case is unwritten." That is wrong.** I read the DPS; the operational detail lives in a different document. [KSK Key Management Procedure v3.4 (2021-09-22)](https://www.iana.org/dnssec/procedures/ksk-operator/KSK_Key_Management_Procedure_v3.4.pdf) **§6.8 "Unavailable RKSH Credential Recovery"** is a full prescribed procedure, ending: *"If an RKSH is no longer able to access their recovery key shares, the recovery key shares should be **considered lost**."* The same document enumerates unplanned ceremony events explicitly — *"Hardware failures · Software failures · CO credentials failure · **Recovery Key Share failure** · **Safe failure** · **Safe deposit box failure** · General facility failure."*
**What survives: the 5-of-7 shares themselves went ~14 years untested.** The scenario was *contemplated*; the path was never *walked*.

**2. The Ceremony 40 lock failure is confirmed, and I had it at 7/20.** Raise to 17/20. The safe had to be **drilled**; it took ~20 hours; ICANN's CSC briefing calls it *"one of the worst-case disaster recovery scenarios that had been contemplated"*; and drilling *"is, in fact, the manufacturer-indicated method."* [Damas, APNIC, 2020-03-04](https://blog.apnic.net/2020/03/04/drilling-for-the-ksk/). **The detail that matters most: three TCRs sat on site for days waiting, because the ceremony could not proceed without them.** *(Date conflict unresolved — IANA's list says 2020-02-16, RIPE Labs says the ceremony ran 2020-02-12, Damas says the safe opened Saturday evening 2020-02-15. The failure and the drilling are not in dispute; the calendar is.)*

**And the sharper Ceremony 41 fact I did not have:** RIPE Labs, ICANN-sourced — ***"Four TCRs transferred their credentials to IANA staff to act as surrogates."***

---

## 5 (EXTENDED) — CIVIC / ORGANIZATIONAL CUSTODY

### 5a. The m-of-n requirement exists — in WebTrust, not the Baseline Requirements
The CA/B Forum BRs are near-empty here (§6.2.2/6.2.3/6.2.4 are headings with no text; §5.2.2 requires only *dual control*). The real rule is [WebTrust for CA Principles and Criteria v2.2.2](https://www.lawtrust.co.za/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/MDS21216WebTrustCA-222FINAL.pdf) *(third-party mirror; currency unverified)*:
- **§3.6.1(d)** — document the *"**m of n rule** where m represents the number of **key shareholders** required to perform an operation and n represents the total number of **key shares**."*
- **§4.4.1** — *"The activation of the CA private signing key is performed using multi-party control (i.e., m of n) with a minimum value of m (e.g., m greater than 2 for Root CAs)."*
- §4.1 requires the ceremony script to carry *"sign-off from participants and witnesses"* and *"notation of any **deviations** from the key generation ceremony script."*
- The **Key Generation Report is a standalone deliverable**, separate from the annual audit — Mozilla tracks them as their own bug class.

### 5b. **THE DECISIVE COMPARATIVE FINDING**

> **Every m-of-n scheme in the entire public-CA and root-zone world is SECRET SHARING — Shamir-style reconstruction — not threshold signing.**

- WebTrust defines m-of-n in terms of *"key shareholders"* and *"key shares."* Reconstruction.
- [Entrust nShield](https://nshielddocs.entrust.com/security-world-docs/v13.4.5/edge-ug/security-worlds.html), the dominant CA HSM: recovery keys are *"split among the cards in the ACS using the same K/N mechanism as for an OCS"*; *"Provided that K is less than N … you have at least K cards available, a hardware security module can **re-create all the keys** stored on the device."* With the warning that matters for your 2-of-3: *"We do not recommend creating card sets in which **K is equal to N** because an error on one card would render the whole card set unusable."* **A hard cliff below K.**
- IANA: 7 shares of one Storage Master Key, reassembled inside an HSM.

**The consequence, stated exactly, and it is the single most useful sentence the sweep produced:**

> In every one of these systems the key is **materialized in one place at activation time**, and the quorum is **a lock on the box, not a distributed signing protocol.** **ICANN's April 2020 workaround — four TCRs couriering their credentials to staff surrogates — is only possible *because* the shares are transferable bearer secrets. Under threshold signing it would be meaningless.**
>
> **Secret sharing degrades gracefully under a pandemic precisely because it can be subverted by courier. Threshold signing cannot be couriered, and therefore cannot bend.**

That is the real tradeoff, and it cuts *against* the direction most of my other evidence pointed. Fork A's assembled-secret is simultaneously its worst security property and its best availability property. **They are the same property.** Fork B's guarantee that no secret ever reconstructs is also a guarantee that **no guardian can ever delegate, courier, or improvise their way through a crisis** — the guardian must personally sign or the rotation does not happen. Whether that is a feature or a fatal rigidity is a values question, and it is yours.

### 5c. Distro archive keys — nobody does threshold signing, and one distro actually lost its key

| System | Signing | Backup / recovery |
|---|---|---|
| **Debian archive** | Single key, **online YubiKey on ftp-master, PIN in a file beside it** | **SECRET SHARING — Shamir 3-of-5** (`gfshare`). Revocation **1-of-5** |
| **Fedora** | Single key per release; **passphrase never stored on any Fedora server** (split by *factor*, not by shares) | None. 2008 recovery = new key + re-sign the entire archive |
| **Red Hat (RHEL)** | Single key | Not published. 2008 response was a **blacklist, not a rotation** |
| **kernel.org** | **No archive key at all.** Per-developer signatures; signed git tags are the anchor | **The web of trust IS the recovery mechanism** — ≥2 existing-member signatures re-admit a re-keyed developer |
| **Arch** | m-of-n **TRUST**, not signing: one maintainer signature per package; that maintainer key needs **≥3 master-key signatures** | Master key replaced on holder departure |
| **Kali** | Single key, **2012–2025, never rotated** | **None. They lost it.** |

**Debian's design lesson is an asymmetry worth stealing: destroying trust is 1-of-5, restoring the key is 3-of-5.** Any single designated revoker can kill the archive key unilaterally; it takes three to bring one back. Verified from dak's production config and [ftp-master.debian.org/keys.html](https://ftp-master.debian.org/keys.html): *"**gfshare** … is used to produce **5 shares of which 3 are needed** to recover the secret key."* Each share is individually PGP-encrypted to its holder; the generator refuses to run unless `GNUPGHOME` is on tmpfs.

**The answer to "has anyone ever actually lost a root/archive key?" — YES. [Kali Linux, 2025-04-28](https://www.kali.org/blog/new-kali-archive-signing-key/):** ***"We lost access to the signing key of the repository, so we had to create a new one."*** Repository frozen from 2025-04-18; **every Kali installation on earth fails `apt update` until the user manually `wget`s the new keyring.** Old key created **2012-03-05 — 13 years, no rotation.** They pre-empt the obvious suspicion: *"So your key was compromised and you don't want to admit it, right?" — "No. As you can see we still include the old key in the keyring; if it was compromised we would have removed it and provided a revocation certificate."* **The entire cryptographic bridge is developers' personal keys signing the new key on a public keyserver.** No secret sharing, no escrow, no revocation certificate. Precedent at the same project: **they let the key expire by accident in 2018** — same custody failure, milder symptom.

**The most instructive pair of decisions in the whole survey — Fedora vs Red Hat, August 2008, one intrusion, opposite calls.** Fedora's [decision mail](https://web.archive.org/web/20210202162244/https://www.redhat.com/archives/fedora-announce-list/2008-August/msg00012.html): ICANN-style split custody saved them — *"the passphrase was not used during the time of the intrusion and is not stored on any of the Fedora servers"* — **and they rotated anyway**, because *"Fedora packages are distributed via multiple third-party mirrors."* Red Hat, facing attackers who had **actually signed OpenSSH packages with a real Red Hat key** ([RHSA-2008:0855](https://access.redhat.com/errata/RHSA-2008:0855)), **did not rotate** — it shipped `openssh-blacklist`. Fedora's [nine-step plan](https://fedoraproject.org/wiki/New_signing_key) took ~5 weeks (Aug 14 → Sept 18), ~3.5 of them with no updates at all, and required re-signing every package. **The bootstrap trick is worth noting: the transitional RPMs were signed by the OLD key `4f2a6fd2`** — the compromised-adjacent key was used one last time to establish trust in its successor. **This is the origin of Fedora's per-release key practice.** The promised post-mortem was never published.

**kernel.org 2011** is the pure Fork-B analogue: no archive key, so recovery was an **N-way developer re-keying** requiring **in-person signatures from as many kernel developers as possible** (phone signing only if you'd testify in court). A revocation-certificate escrow was considered and **never built**. The promised incident report was never delivered, and *the promise itself was quietly deleted from kernel.org between 2013-03-01 and 2013-03-02* ([Ars, 2013](https://arstechnica.com/information-technology/2013/09/who-rooted-kernel-org-servers-two-years-ago-how-did-it-happen-and-why/)). Today's [governing principle](https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/process/maintainer-pgp-guide.html): ***"trust must always be placed with developers and never with the code hosting infrastructure."***

> **The generalization: nobody does threshold *signing*.** Every archive is signed by one online key. Where m-of-n appears it is in one of two adjacent places — **recovering** a lost secret (Debian 3-of-5), or **admitting** a key to the trust set (Debian 2 DDs, kernel.org 2 account holders, Arch 3 master keys). And every recovery in the record has the same shape: **mint a new key, get humans to sign it out of band, re-sign or re-point the archive, and push the new anchor through the old key if it still works — by hand if it doesn't.**

---

## 7 (EXTENDED) — CEREMONY FAILURE, MEASURED: 19 CASES

**The dominant public-CA failure mode is not lost quorum. It is the script and the executed reality diverging, undetected, for months.**

| CA | Bug / date | What went wrong |
|---|---|---|
| **IdenTrust** | [1895006](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1895006), 2024-05-03 | **Generated a self-signed ROOT when they meant an intermediate.** *"A PKI operator entered the wrong command parameter"* and *"The ceremony witness missed noticing the invocation of the wrong command."* **Fix: split the tool into two binaries so the wrong object type is not selectable mid-ceremony.** |
| **SecureTrust** | [1671037](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1671037), 2020-10 | **The ceremony video recorder failed; the recording was unrecoverable.** CPS required 7-year retention. **All 7 intermediates were revoked.** The ceremony was fine; the *proof* wasn't, and the keys were thrown away anyway. |
| **Camerfirma** | [1532333](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1532333), 2019-03 | A **stale template file** carried the wrong serial number into a CRL ceremony → **revoked the wrong intermediate CA**, disrupting Portuguese electronic payments. |
| **SwissSign** | [1506607](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1506607), 2018-11 | *"The organizationIdentifier had been set correctly in the key ceremony protocol"* — but mistyped in the GUI and copy-pasted across five more CAs. **The canonical script-vs-reality divergence.** ~17 months undetected. |
| **iTrusChina** | [1923279](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1923279), 2024-10 | **Root key ceremony held 2018-07-31 with no Key Generation Ceremony audit report at all** — *"due to our inexperience."* **Undetected for six years**, found by the Chrome Root Program, not by any auditor. |
| **GlobalSign** | [2058503](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2058503), 2026-07 | subCA created with the **EV Code Signing OID instead of OV TLS** (a missing ".10"). Passed *"standard multi-person review"* because reviewers and linters checked CA/B OIDs, not internal CPS OIDs. **~10 months undetected.** |
| **Let's Encrypt** | [2038351](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2038351), 2026-05, **still open** | **Three non-compliant cross-certificates issued as part of the 2025-09-03 "Gen Y" root ceremony**, reported by a third party ~8 months later. Causes: no lint coverage for cross-cert rules, *"compliance review fatigue,"* and the rule living in CCADB Policy rather than the BRs. **The CA with the best ceremony tooling in the industry still emitted three defective CA certs.** |
| **DigiCert** | [1684442](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1684442), 2020-12 | SHA-1 intermediate created in a ceremony, banned since 2016. Linters *"evaluate the individual certificate being signed"* — **not the signer/signee relationship** — so a ceremony can emit a policy-violating cert while every automated check passes. |
| **FNMT** | [1626805](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1626805), 2020-04 | Auditor finding: *"Unsafe storage of access codes in PKI Data Centre"* — **activation material co-located with the modules, defeating split custody.** |
| **GlobalSign** | [1591005](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1591005), 2020/2021 | **Deliberate key-DESTRUCTION ceremonies.** They destroyed timestamping-CA private keys rather than revoke, because revocation would invalidate historical signatures. Qualified auditors witnessed and issued **ISAE 3000 reports attesting genuine destruction.** |

**And the one where the offline root's failure mode was the keyboard.** Roger Grimes, ["My Key Signing Ceremony Gone Wrong"](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/my-key-signing-ceremony-gone-wrong-roger-grimes) *(single-source, ~2015, unverifiable)*: Fortune-500 root CA update, C-levels and auditors and legal and both split-knowledge password holders in the room. Boot password fails. Windows password fails. Root cause: ***"a laptop sitting in a safe for five years undisturbed had its 1 key on the keyboard go bad"*** — and both password halves contained a "1."

**Golden principles this yields, each earned by an incident:**
- **Make the wrong operation unselectable, not merely discouraged** (IdenTrust split the binary).
- **Lint the test certificates generated with the *identical production ceremony settings*, inside the ceremony** (GoDaddy [1777128](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1777128)).
- **Fold compliance sign-off into the ceremony script itself** (DigiCert [1563573](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1563573)).
- **Losing the evidence is as fatal as losing the key** (SecureTrust revoked 7 intermediates over a dead camcorder).
- **Publish rehearsal artifacts in advance** — [`letsencrypt/ceremony-demos`](https://github.com/letsencrypt/ceremony-demos) was created **2020-08-13**, before the September 2020 ISRG Root X2 ceremony. *(Caveat: the demo configs are public; the production YAMLs and logs are not.)*

### The negative result, and it is load-bearing
**After direct Bugzilla phrase-substring searching across the whole CA Program product — `"key custodian"`, `"quorum of"`, `"shares of"`, `"ceremony was aborted"`, `"zeroized"` — there is NO public-CA incident of lost activation shares, a lost HSM quorum card set, or an unreachable keyholder causing an inability to sign.** *(In modern CA bugs, "quorum" means MPIC validation quorum, not HSM quorum.)*

**Best read: this class of failure happens — Grimes is one, Kali is another — but it is only *reportable* to root programs if it causes a missed CRL or a misissuance, so it is systematically absent from the public record.** The only real quorum-unavailability event in public PKI is **ICANN Ceremony 41**; the only physical lockout is **Ceremony 40**. Also not found: any incident of a CA **accidentally** destroying or bricking a root key — only deliberate, audited destructions.

---

## THE SWEEP, IN ONE PARAGRAPH

Across five research streams, **not one documented failure in the entire public record turned on Shamir-versus-multisig.** They turned on: quorum counts that weren't counts of distinct signers (four sightings, one a recurrence in the same library six years later); custody that was correlated in ways nobody re-checked (Ronin's 5-of-9 that was 1-of-1, Loopring's guardians-of-guardians, FNMT's access codes stored beside the HSM); ceremonies whose script and reality diverged undetected for six months to six years; guardians who forgot, went dark, or folded to a warm phone call; and vendors who left. **The two forks differ most in a place neither branch names: Fork A's assembled secret is a bearer token that can be couriered, delegated, and improvised with — which is simultaneously its worst security property and the only reason ICANN could hold a ceremony during a pandemic. Fork B cannot bend, in either direction.** And the finding that changes the shape rather than answering it: under KERI's own rules, **a non-delegated rotation cannot supersede another rotation** — so whichever fork you take, a compromise of the recovery authority is terminal unless the identifier is delegated. That is a third axis, and on severity it dominates the one you asked about.
